# 5. Agent Skills

The repository should contain portable, task-specific development guidance
with separate contributor and consumer audiences:

```text
agents/
├── catalog.json
├── skills/
│   ├── mdk-knowledge-maintenance/SKILL.md
│   ├── mdk-network-knowledge/SKILL.md
│   ├── mdk-contract-knowledge/SKILL.md
│   └── mdk-*/SKILL.md
└── consumer/
    ├── APP_AGENTS.template.md
    └── skills/
        └── mdk-*/SKILL.md
```

Each directory basename should equal its globally unique frontmatter `name`.
The text source should use the portable Agent Skills core rather than requiring
vendor-specific metadata. `agents/catalog.json` supplies audience and domain
routing without duplicating the procedure.

Each `SKILL.md` should have precise discovery metadata and a small procedural
body with the relevant subset of this common structure:

```text
Purpose

When to use this skill

Relevant repository areas

Canonical sources

Architecture rules

Preferred implementation patterns

Forbidden / discouraged patterns

Verification commands

Common mistakes

Examples
```

Skills should explain **how to work inside this repository**, while policy
stays in maintained standards and canonical technical facts remain in
`knowledge/` and package documentation. A knowledge-maintenance skill routes to
the common standard and selected domain skill; it does not repeat their full
contents.

Consumer skills explain how to build **with released MDK APIs** and must not
ship contributor maintenance procedures. Agent-specific discovery roots are
materialized from the selected audience by copying skill directories unchanged;
they are not separately authored or recompiled prompt trees. Static text skills
must remain usable without MCP. MCP is an optional adapter for capabilities
that are genuinely live, remote, authenticated, or structured.

