# 7. Knowledge Architecture

Knowledge is a maintained development asset with separate human, machine,
agent, evidence, and executable surfaces. The universal v0.4 contract is
recorded in ADR-0006 and the knowledge-management standard.

```text
knowledge/
├── README.md           # human/domain router
├── AGENTS.md           # concise subtree agent rules
├── schema/             # shared executable contracts
├── networks/
├── contracts/
├── protocols/
├── workflows/
├── troubleshooting/
└── security/
```

Each knowledge module has two required entry points:

```text
README.md               human orientation and domain routing
index.json               machine routing and physical-path resolution
```

Other content is grouped by responsibility and created only when needed:

```text
records/                 canonical machine-readable facts
sources/                 pinned source catalogs
evidence/                bounded observations and reproductions
fixtures/                deterministic validation inputs
schema/                  domain structural schemas
review/                  candidates, gaps, conflicts, review packets
artifacts/               raw canonical inputs such as ABIs
generated/               colocated derived output
```

This organization is universal by role, not by file extension or identical
domain content. The root `index.json` intentionally remains outside the role
folders. Empty directories are not part of conformance.

Maintained JSON records use a common envelope that identifies:

```text
schema version
stable kind and ID
status
owner
support status
review status
verification and review-after times
scope
limitations
```

Evidence maturity, MDK support, and human review are independent states. A
verified observation is not automatically supported or accepted. Domain
records may extend the envelope without changing these meanings.

Cross-domain identity uses stable module, resource, and optional record IDs.
The target module index resolves physical paths, so moving a file is not a
breaking fact change. JSON Pointer may select a value inside a record; relative
paths must not serve as cross-domain identity.

The intended flow is:

```text
verified source
      ↓
canonical knowledge
      ↓
docs / SDK / skills / examples
```

Documentation, SDK behavior and agent instructions should not develop independent versions of the same protocol facts.

JSON Schema owns local structural shape. Dependency-free semantic validators
own cross-record resolution, evidence sufficiency, digests, freshness,
generated drift, and protocol invariants. Passing either layer does not supply
missing evidence, review, or support.

Canonical knowledge is a maintained, validated projection of evidence. It is
not self-authenticating and must never outrank the evidence it cites. For a
claim about what a particular deployment actually does, block-pinned on-chain
state and bytecode together with version-matched verified source take
precedence over explanatory or product documentation. Documentation may lag a
deployment; when it conflicts, retain it as discrepancy evidence, scope the
deployed fact to its observed version/block, and derive internal docs from that
fact. Do not rewrite evidence to preserve an older narrative.

Generated human reference exists for readability and declares its canonical
inputs. Generators are deterministic and drift-checked; generated output is
never manually maintained as another authority.

Migration to v0.4 was completed one module at a time. Stable IDs and evidence
were preserved while index mappings and legacy path references changed. Records
were re-verified only when freshness, conflict, missing evidence, or domain risk
required it. The root catalog and all-v0.4 validation are now required.

