# 15. Transaction Standard

State-changing operations should follow a consistent lifecycle:

```text
validate
   ↓
resolve deployment
   ↓
read / quote
   ↓
build typed request
   ↓
simulate
   ↓
approve if required
   ↓
revalidate / resimulate
   ↓
submit
   ↓
track
   ↓
confirm
   ↓
reconcile
```

The toolkit should distinguish:

```text
constructed
simulated
submitted
included
confirmed
reconciled
```

A transaction hash is not equivalent to successful protocol execution.
Only the reconciled state represents protocol success. Approval and action
hashes remain distinct; timeout is non-terminal, replacement links a separately
tracked successor, and a reorg returns the action to tracking. Each protocol
writer owns volatile quote inputs, allowed partial outcomes, receipt events,
post-state checks, and mismatch behavior. Cross-chain reconciliation continues
through provider message and destination-delivery evidence.

Shared errors should cover common cases such as:

```text
UnsupportedNetwork
MissingDeployment
ChainMismatch
InvalidUnits
StaleQuote
ProviderError
ContractRevert
ApprovalError
TransactionReplaced
TransactionFailed
ReconciliationError
```

